    "q-279595": {
      "dom_id": "step-q279595",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "If $X∼B(3,\\frac{2}{3}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X=1)≈${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(3,\\frac{2}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3-1} \\\\  & =3{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.222\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
