    "q-279687": {
      "dom_id": "step-q279687",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "If $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X>2)≈${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4-4} \\\\  & =4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}+1{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.313\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
