    "q-129985": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129985",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Given that $X∼B(11,0.4),$ compute $P(X>1).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(11,0.4),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{x})(0.4)^{x}(0.6)^{11-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X>1)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=2,3,…,11.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\le 1)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{0})(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{11-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{1})(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{11-1} \\\\  & =1(0.4)^{0}(0.6)^{11}+11(0.4)^{1}(0.6)^{10} \\\\  & ≈0.0302\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & =1-P(X\\le 1) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0302 \\\\  & =0.9698\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.",
